{{Nihongo|'''Sanesair Co., Ltd.'''<!--Name change cited below--> ('''SAA''')|山軽航空|Sankei-Kōkū}}, is an airline headquartered in [[Chiyoda, Okami|Chiyoda]],<!--The Ward is a municipality, so it needs to be stated in the introduction!--> [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]].<!--HQ info Cited below--> Its main hubs are Okami's Air Island (Ikoshima International Airport) and Okami International Airport (Tokuyama Itazaki Airport), as well as [[Toyozawa]]'s [[Shirakawa International Airport]]. The airline and two of its subsidiaries (O-Air, Sanesexpress) are members of the Adonian Arrow [[wikipedia:airline alliance|airline alliance]].

SAA group companies include '''Sanesair''' for international and domestic services; Sanesexpress for international and domestic low-cost services; O-Air for domestic feeder services; and Sanesair Cargo for cargo and mail services. SAA group operations include scheduled and non-scheduled international and domestic [[passenger]] and cargo services to 220 destinations in 35 countries worldwide, including [[wikipedia:codeshare agreement|codeshares]]. The group has a fleet of 130 aircraft. In the fiscal year ended 31 March 2009, the airline group carried over 52 million passengers and over 1.1 million tons of cargo and mail. By 2016, however, it had taken over [[Okaiken Air]] as Okaiken's largest airline, with more orders to its existing fleet of aircraft.

Sanesair was established in 1956 and became the unofficial second [[wikipedia:flag carrier|national airline]] of Okaiken. After over three decades of service and expansion, the airline was fully privatized in 1987. Sanesair is currently an official sponsor of [[Okaiken Football Association]] and the [[Okaiken national football team]]. The other major airline of Okaiken, [[Okaiken Air]], is Sanesair' main rival with fierce competition between the two airlines. In July 2015, the two airlines were officially recognized as the two flag carriers of Okaiken, with a new livery presented in October 2015 to reflect the new status

==History==
The operational history of Sanesair is deeply intertwined with the broader socio-political evolution of Okaiken, beginning with the nation's emergence from the post-war occupation period. Following the formal end of the Great Adonian War on April 28, 1952, and the subsequent restoration of Sanese sovereignty, the domestic environment was characterized by intense debates over military spending and the role of the Imperial House. Sanesair was founded on 2 December 1955, coinciding with the consolidation of fragmented political factions into the Progressive Party of Okaiken and the Okaiken Democratic Party. During this formative period, the airline served as a critical infrastructure link as the government began rehabilitating thousands of officials purged during the occupation and established the Okaiken Self-Defense Forces under civilian control in 1954.

The airline entered a phase of rapid expansion in 1961 with the rise of Prime Minister Honda Tadashige, whose administration architected the Sanese Economic Miracle. Under the ambitious Income Doubling Plan, the government funded massive infrastructure projects, including the Shinkansen network, which functioned alongside Sanesair to modernize the national logistics framework. This era of stability and unprecedented growth lasted until the mid-1960s, when the airline's trajectory was disrupted by the onset of the Quiet Revolution. The external partition of Kwangju and the subsequent spike in resource prices led to rapid inflation and the catastrophic Black Monday stock exchange crash on October 15, 1967.

During the turbulent period between 1968 and 1970, Sanesair navigated the transition from the Mitoyagi Empire to the Federal Republic of Okaiken. Following the declaration of the Republic on January 1, 1970, the newly formed government under Yoshimizu Ohno implemented strict austerity measures to manage national debt, including the seizure of Imperial assets to fund recovery. The subsequent Great Transformation of the 1970s saw the airline adapt to a republican identity as the state worked to dismantle Imperial ideology. This era concluded with the Sanese Recovery, during which government bureaucrats shifted their focus toward deregulation and foreign investment, setting the stage for the explosive prosperity of the next decade.

The 1980s, known culturally as the Go-go 8090 era, represented the zenith of the airline's financial growth alongside the soaring asset prices of the Bubble Economy. Under the administrative reforms of Prime Minister Junichiro Okazaki, the integration of Okaiken into the global economy reached a fever pitch, with the Okami Stock Exchange hitting record highs by 1990. However, the 1991 collapse of the bubble initiated the Lost Decade of stagnation and deflation. Sanesair was forced to weather the Second Lost Decade of the early 2000s, a period marked by structural crises, corporate malfeasance, and the 2004 Ise Prefecture Earthquakes which exposed widespread negligence in public infrastructure.
[[File:Sanesair Aircraft Taking Off at Toyozawa Airport.png|250px|thumb|left|A Sanesair A320 aircraft taking off at Toyozawa Airport.]]
The modern era of the carrier began with the technocratic pivot initiated by the Digital Infrastructure Act of 2008. This landmark legislation diverted billions into projects like the Yagikoshu Data Corridor, facilitating the integration of the Satori network and AI-driven algorithmic governance into airline operations. This transition occurred alongside the Jutsu Restoration of 2010 to 2016, which saw the return of the Monarchy and a nationalist backlash against perceived liberal failures. Following a five-year social shift under the communist administration of Sho Aoyagi, which emphasized labor rights and social safety nets, the airline currently operates under the 2025 coalition government of the Progressive and Socialist parties. This current phase is defined by stable progressivism and the implementation of massive social platforms, such as the National Universal Health Act of 2023.

==Corporate affairs and identity==

===Livery and branding===
Sanesair’s corporate identity is characterized by its signature "Crimson" color palette, a visual choice designed to project boldness, energy, and national pride within the East Fosian Sphere. The current livery, unveiled in October 2015 following the airline's official designation as a flag carrier, features a clean, minimalist white fuselage contrasted by a vibrant red tail fin. The vertical stabilizer prominently displays the airline’s logo—a stylized, geometric white orchid petals against a red field—which serves as a modern homage to Sanese botanical heritage and the "Socialist Connectivity" roots of the Republican era.

The airline’s wordmark, "sanesair," is rendered in a custom lowercase sans-serif font in deep red along the forward section of the fuselage. This branding is consistently applied across all passenger touchpoints, from the "Sanesair Crimson Lounges" in hub airports to the digital interfaces of the "Avatar" in-flight entertainment system. The airline’s primary slogan, ''"Fly Red, Fly Sanesair,"'' reinforces this visual identity and aims to evoke a sense of reliability and passion for service among both domestic and international travelers.

===Fleet naming convention===
Unlike many of its regional competitors, Sanesair utilizes a highly structured and culturally significant naming convention for its fleet. This system, which has been in place since 1955, categorizes aircraft names based on their size and operational role:

Strategic Flagships: Large widebody aircraft, such as the Airbus A380 and Boeing 777-9, are named after Okaiken’s largest historical provinces from the Sengoku period, such as ''Sado'', ''Shinano'', and ''Dewa''.

Medium-sized planes in the international long-haul fleet are named after famous historical figures of Okaiken, including the unifiers Ogasawara Terumasa and Honda Shigetada, Smaller aircraft are named after Sanese birds or cultural concepts. Notably, the A350-900 known as ''Planey Mcplaneface'' was retained as a permanent part of the fleet identity following a viral naming competition in 2016, despite initial administrative pushback. The aircraft reserved for the Imperial family and government officials bear the name ''Mitoyagi'', referring to the nation's archaic title, while the primary backup aircraft is named ''Tanchōzuru'' (Red-crowned crane).

===Lounge network===
The airline operates a tiered lounge system that mirrors its "Fly Red" identity. The Sanesair Crimson Lounge, located at major hubs like Okami-Tobu and Toyozawa Shirakawa, is the flagship offering for first-class and high-tier frequent flyers. For international business travelers, the Sanesair Red Lounge provides standardized premium amenities across Fosian gateways. Domestically, the Mican Lounge (named after a famous Sanese citrus fruit) serves passengers in regional airports, emphasizing local hospitality and products.

